    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Insurance companies evaluate hail damage in a set order. The adjuster confirms that hail hit the property by checking storm data and soft metals, counts hail hits inside a test square on each roof slope, decides whether those hits are functional damage or wear, and then builds an estimate in pricing software. Each step involves judgment calls, and each one is a place where your own evidence can change the result.</p>
<p>Here's what happens at each stage and what you can do about it.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Was there a storm?</h2>
<p>Before anyone climbs a ladder, the claim file usually includes a weather check. Insurers use radar-based hail reports and storm databases to see whether hail of a meaningful size was likely at your address on the date of loss you reported. If their data shows no hail, or only small hail, the adjuster arrives expecting to find nothing.</p>
<p><strong>Your counter:</strong> report the right date. Look up NOAA storm reports for your area with our <a href="/tools/storm-report-lookup/">storm report lookup</a> and include them with the claim. If neighbors had roofs replaced after the same storm, note it. A wrong date of loss is one of the easiest ways to lose a hail claim, because the insurer's weather data won't match.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Collateral damage and soft metals</h2>
<p>The adjuster next walks around the house looking at what's called collateral damage: dents and marks on things other than the roof. Gutters, downspouts, window screens, the A/C condenser, painted fences, mailboxes, vehicles in the driveway, and roof vents and flashings all count. The adjuster is answering three questions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Did hail hit here?</strong> No dents anywhere makes roof damage hard to accept.</li>
<li><strong>How big was it?</strong> The size of dents in soft aluminum gives a rough idea of hailstone size.</li>
<li><strong>Which way was it moving?</strong> Dents on one side of the house and not the other show storm direction, which tells the adjuster which roof slopes should have the most damage.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Your counter:</strong> photograph collateral damage before the inspection, with something for scale, on every side of the house. If an adjuster says "I don't see much on the gutters," you have photos showing otherwise. Our <a href="/post/how-to-identify-hail-damage-by-roof-type/">guide to hail damage by roof type</a> shows what soft-metal evidence looks like and how chalk makes dents visible.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Test squares and hit counts</h2>
<p>On a shingle roof, the core of the evaluation is the test square. The adjuster marks off an area on each slope, commonly 10 feet by 10 feet (one roofing "square"), and circles each hail hit inside it with chalk. The count per slope, compared with the carrier's internal guideline, drives the decision for that slope: replace, repair a few shingles, or nothing.</p>
<p>That threshold is not in your policy and not in Texas law. It's a carrier guideline, it varies by company and roof type, and it isn't always disclosed. What matters for you is how the square was placed and what was counted.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Was a square drawn on every slope, including small ones and the back of the house?</li>
<li>Was the square on a representative part of the slope, or on a sheltered area near a wall or under a tree?</li>
<li>Were hits on ridge caps, hips and vents counted or ignored?</li>
<li>Were bruises counted, or only visible fractures and granule loss?</li>
<li>Did the adjuster dismiss hits as blisters, scuffs or "mechanical damage"?</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Your counter:</strong> have your own inspector or public adjuster mark squares on every slope, photograph them at roof level with the chalk circles visible, and keep a slope-by-slope count. When counts differ, specific photos of specific hits win the argument, not general statements that the roof is damaged.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask the adjuster at the end of the inspection, "Which slopes are you approving, and how many hits did you count on each?" Write the answer down. It's much easier to dispute a specific number than a vague conclusion in a letter weeks later.</p></div>

<h2>Step 4: Functional damage or not</h2>
<p>A hit counts only if the adjuster decides it's functional damage. On shingles that usually means a fracture of the mat, a bruise, or granule loss deep enough to expose asphalt. On metal it means punctures, coating damage, or seam and fastener damage, especially if your policy has a cosmetic damage exclusion; see <a href="/post/cosmetic-vs-functional-hail-damage/">cosmetic vs. functional hail damage</a>. On tile it means cracks and breaks.</p>
<p>This is where "wear and tear" enters. The adjuster may attribute marks to age, blistering, thermal cracking, poor ventilation or foot traffic. A roof can have both age-related wear and hail damage; the insurer isn't entitled to treat one as the other.</p>
<p><strong>Your counter:</strong> show the difference. Hail hits are random, circular, and concentrated on storm-facing slopes; wear is uniform and follows sun exposure or drainage. Close-ups with a scale reference, taken soon after the storm, are your best evidence.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Engineers and standardized inspection methods</h2>
<p>When a claim is large, disputed, or involves metal, tile or commercial roofs, the insurer may hire an engineer. Many engineers and adjusters train in standardized hail-inspection methods (Haag Engineering's courses are among the best known). These methods focus on distinguishing hail damage from manufacturing defects and weathering, often by examining a sample of shingles, measuring dents, and comparing damage with the hail size the engineer believes fell.</p>
<p>An engineer's report carries weight with the adjuster, but it's one opinion. Reports sometimes rely on a hail size estimate from weather data rather than the dents at the property, inspect only a sample of slopes, or reach conclusions about wear without explaining what was observed.</p>
<p><strong>Your counter:</strong> ask for a copy of the full report and the engineer's photos. Be present at the inspection. Compare the report's hail size estimate with your soft-metal measurements and NOAA reports. If the report is wrong on facts, respond in writing point by point with photos.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Step 6: The photo report</h2>
<p>The adjuster's file usually includes a photo report: captioned photos of the elevations, test squares, collateral damage and any interior damage. It's what a desk adjuster or supervisor sees when approving the claim, often without ever visiting the property. If a slope isn't photographed, it may as well not exist.</p>
<p><strong>Your counter:</strong> request the photo report along with the estimate. Look for slopes, elevations or rooms that are missing. If your own photos show damage the report omits, send them with a short cover letter listing what was left out.</p>

<h2>Step 7: The software estimate</h2>
<p>Most insurers write property estimates in Xactimate or similar software, which uses regional unit prices updated over time. The estimate lists each item with a quantity and price, applies depreciation, and subtracts your deductible. The software prices whatever the adjuster enters, so the estimate is only as complete as the scope.</p>
<p>Common scope gaps on roof estimates include missing slopes, ridge cap and starter, drip edge, flashing, vents and pipe jacks, steep and high-roof charges, extra shingle layers, code-required items, and overhead and profit. Depreciation is another judgment call: the roof age and depreciation rate the adjuster chooses can move the first payment significantly.</p>
<p><strong>Your counter:</strong> compare the carrier's estimate line by line with your roofer's bid or an independent Xactimate estimate. Our article on an <a href="/post/underpaid-hail-damage-insurance-claim/">underpaid hail damage claim</a> covers how to dispute a short estimate, and items discovered during tear-off are handled through a <a href="/post/hail-damage-supplement-claim/">supplement</a>.</p>

<h2>What adjusters weigh and how to respond</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Insurer looks at</th><th>Common problem</th><th>Your evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Storm data for the date of loss</td><td>Wrong date reported, or radar shows small hail</td><td>NOAA storm reports, neighbors' claims, dated photos</td></tr>
<tr><td>Soft metals and collateral damage</td><td>Dents overlooked or attributed to old storms</td><td>Scaled photos of dents on every side, chalked</td></tr>
<tr><td>Test squares</td><td>Squares on sheltered areas, slopes skipped, low counts</td><td>Your own squares on every slope with roof-level photos</td></tr>
<tr><td>Functional vs. cosmetic or wear</td><td>Hits called blisters, scuffs or age</td><td>Close-ups with scale; pattern and direction of hits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Engineer report</td><td>Sampled slopes, assumed hail size</td><td>Attend; written point-by-point response with photos</td></tr>
<tr><td>Software estimate</td><td>Missing line items, high depreciation</td><td>Independent Xactimate estimate and line-by-line comparison</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Be at the inspection</h2>
<p>The single most useful thing you can do is be present, or have someone there who knows roofs. Insurers' adjusters are often handling many claims after a big storm and have limited time on each roof. Having someone point out specific hits, slopes and components changes what goes into the file. We attend carrier inspections and re-inspections on the claims we handle, and our <a href="/services/roof-hail-damage-claims/">roof and hail damage claims</a> page explains how we document a roof before the insurer arrives.</p>
